    {
      "id": "tide-fac-17",
      "name": "Lean: Unleashed Destruction",
      "shortName": "Ephemeral Copy Engine",
      "displayName": "Fleeting Mirage",
      "displayDescription": "Conjures event copies and cashes them in for high-spark advantage.",
      "summary": "A warrior tribal tide that builds a self-sustaining board loop by triggering on warrior dissolution to return warriors from the void to hand, fueled by abandon-for-energy and amplified by persistent warrior-count anthem effects — distinguished from its closest sibling by doubling down on multiple redundant dissolution-return triggers rather than grow-and-burst dissolution payoffs.",
      "description": "This tide is almost entirely composed of warriors (43 of 45 copies), built around a repeating dissolution-return loop: Cinderblade Legionnaire and Starbound Striker each appear at two copies and both trigger on dissolution to return another warrior from the void to hand, and Burning Revenant extends this further by returning a lesser-cost warrior from the void whenever any allied warrior is dissolved. Blood Reaver at two copies provides the energy engine, turning an abandoned warrior into 2●, which funds the loop and feeds Spellweaver's ability to convert abandoned warriors into 1✦ figments. With a persistent board of warriors established, the tide scales hard through anthem effects: Inspiring Templar grants +1✦ per allied warrior to itself and its allies, Dreadmount Sovereign reduces its own cost per warrior and uses Support to hand out +3✦, and Assault Leader buffs every allied warrior each Dawn. Eclipse Herald serves as an in-faction removal piece, storing ⧗ charges to dissolve enemies of matching size, which can also feed the dissolution-return triggers. Compared to tide-fac-12, which anchors on Burning Revenant at two copies but leans into grow-and-burst dissolution payoffs via Inferno's Herald and mass resurrection through Wake the Fallen, this tide prioritizes redundancy across three distinct dissolution-return triggers to keep the loop running at low spark thresholds; compared to tide-fac-29, which shares Blood Reaver as its highest-copy card but channels abandonment primarily into figment-token production and cost reduction, this tide treats dissolution and void-return as the central engine rather than a supporting tool.",
      "color": "yellow",
      "claims": {"tribe": "events", "mechanics": ["discard","reclaim"]},
      "role": "facet",
      "leanCardId": "006e2f95-59a2-4cda-a777-e64f4aa31060",
      "cards": [
        {"id":"08fe3190-f559-4152-941d-33ec4180f509","name":"Data Pulse","text":"Gain 2●. Draw a card.","copies":2},
        {"id":"4cec92f2-9bac-4949-a602-cd0a44618aaf","name":"The Ringleader","subtype":"Visitor","text":"▸Materialized: The next time you play an event this turn, create a 0● ephemeral copy of that event in your hand.","copies":2},
        {"id":"006e2f95-59a2-4cda-a777-e64f4aa31060","name":"Unleashed Destruction","text":"Dissolve a ≤2● cost enemy.\n\nReclaim 5●.","copies":1},
        {"id":"01d53e49-7833-4122-a970-b72c73ad2c28","name":"Crimson Pilgrimage","text":"Look at the top 4 cards of your deck. Choose one: Draw 1 of the cards, or draw 2 of the cards and they gain ephemeral.","copies":1},
